# P12-08 Canary split: production hostname unchanged, traffic selected by client-key prefix.
#
# This is a fragment, not a whole Caddyfile. It replaces exactly one existing site block on the
# server; every other site (cpam, grok, kiro, sub) stays untouched.
#
# Deliberately NO global `servers` block. Caddy scopes `servers` by listener address, not by site,
# so a global timeouts block on the server's single :443 listener would silently apply to every
# other site sharing it. Scoping it would require moving this site to another port, which changes
# the public surface.
#
# That is safe to omit because Caddy's server-level timeouts default to none, which is exactly what
# a long-lived event stream needs. `CR-P12-ROLLOUT-001` requires read/idle timeouts above the data
# plane's 15s SSE keepalive interval; "no timeout" satisfies that by construction. The live config
# on the server currently sets no timeouts, verified with `caddy adapt`.
#
# The hazard is therefore the opposite of the usual one: adding a routine read or idle timeout here
# would cut healthy idle streams between keepalives. `scripts/check-p12-caddy-split.rb` enforces
# that any timeout introduced later still clears the gateway's own ceilings, which it reads from the
# Rust source (15s keepalive, 30s inbound body, 15min progress deadline, 1h streaming total).
#
# Caddy needs no explicit flush configuration for SSE: responses with
# `Content-Type: text/event-stream` are flushed immediately regardless of flush_interval.
# Do not add `encode` to this site — compressing an event stream reintroduces buffering.

cpa.example.invalid {
	# The split matches a non-secret fixed literal prefix. No key value ever appears in this file.
	# The data plane accepts either header, mutually exclusively, so both are routed.
	@canary_bearer {
		header Authorization "Bearer rgw_*"
	}
	@canary_api_key {
		header X-Api-Key "rgw_*"
	}

	handle @canary_bearer {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18180
	}

	handle @canary_api_key {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18180
	}

	# Everything else keeps reaching the incumbent CPA unchanged.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8317
	}
}

# There is deliberately no site block for the management listener. 127.0.0.1:18181 must remain
# unreachable from the public internet; adding a route here would break that invariant.
